WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.440
- We did it, Reddit!

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.320
Nvidia was so embarrassed by the specs

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.460
of the RTX 4080 12 gig
that they unlaunched it

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.960
and now in its place
we've got the RTX 4070 TI.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.430
It's the same GPU but
with a different name.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.330
Oh.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.792
But it got a $100 price drop,

00:00:20.792 --> 00:00:24.330
which unfortunately isn't quite the W

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.010
that you might think it is.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.750
While theoretically there
will be 4070 TIs for $700,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.320
there are no first party
founder's edition cards,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.830
meaning that that MSRP
is really just that,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:41.760
a manufacturer's suggested retail price.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.350
And later we're gonna get into

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.544
why no one really follows
Nvidia's suggestions anymore.

00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:49.230
The card that we were sent
for review, for example,

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.880
is lightly overclocked
and costs 850 US dollars,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.430
meaning that I kind of
have to evaluate this

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:01.430
as a 4080 12 gig with a name
change and a 5.5% discount.

00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:06.169
This thing better be amazing.

00:01:06.169 --> 00:01:08.310
Amazing like our sponsor!

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.240
- Ridge Wallet.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.030
Ridge Wallet has redefined
the traditional wallet

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.180
with its compact frame
and RFID blocking plates.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.510
Keep your wallet bulged down
and use our offer code Linus

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.520
to save 10% and get
free worldwide shipping.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.020
(upbeat electronic music)

00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:33.900
- Right outta the gate,
the 4070 TI is in trouble

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.110
with specs that I might
have imagined on a 4070

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.040
or even a 4060 Super that
puts its total bandwidth

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.660
somewhere in between the
RTX 3070 and 3070 TI.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.930
So it's only chance is if it's other specs

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.138
can pick up the slack.

00:01:50.138 --> 00:01:54.360
Fortunately on paper, the
4070 TI's core performance

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.820
should contribute to a sizable uplift.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.940
It has 25% more CUDA
course than its predecessor

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.520
and they're clocked substantially higher.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.260
That's a good sign even before

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.580
the architectural improvements

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.800
thanks to its Ada Lovelace cores.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.620
This means improved ray
tracing and tensor cores

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.840
and shader execution reordering,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.839
which optimizes the order of the data

00:02:14.839 --> 00:02:17.160
sent to the ray-tracing cores.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.045
Nvidia called this as big
an innovation for GPUs

00:02:21.045 --> 00:02:24.480
as out of order execution was for CPUs,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.090
though if our recent
poll is any indication,

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.210
most of you won't be making
use of it anytime soon.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.200
Of course, how would you,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.779
given the ongoing popularity
of the 10 series GPUs,

00:02:33.779 --> 00:02:37.650
which are turning seven
years old this year.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.560
Nvidia's marketing push for the 4070 TI

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.450
is that it makes for a great upgrade

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.777
from 10 and 20 series
GPUs, which okay, Nvidia,

00:02:46.777 --> 00:02:49.110
let's play along with that for a moment.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.158
Compared to the GTX 1080 and 1080 TI 4K,

00:02:52.158 --> 00:02:55.320
we are looking at a
staggering two to three times

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.030
improvement in "Shadow of the Tomb Raider"

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.967
taking us from sub 60-FPSs
averages to over a hundred.

00:03:00.967 --> 00:03:03.127
"Modern Warfare 2" is even more impressive

00:03:03.127 --> 00:03:06.240
at up to four and a half
times the performance.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.934
We're talking cinematic to
playable (snaps) just like that.

00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:13.584
1440p does give our 10
series cards a better chance,

00:03:13.584 --> 00:03:17.070
but we're still looking at two
to four times the performance

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.450
in both of these games,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.610
making this truly an incredible upgrade

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.140
over the 10 series GPU.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.898
No, no, no, no, this is stupid.

00:03:23.898 --> 00:03:28.898
I don't need to spend $850 to
upgrade a seven year old card.

00:03:28.987 --> 00:03:30.834
Ah, that's better.

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:33.150
To see how it really competes,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:37.140
we tested the RTX 4070 TI,
not against what Nvidia wants,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.950
but against a truly
intimidating stack of GPUs.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.590
As an FYI, even though
our review was so recent,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.413
we sanity checked our RX
7000 series GPU results

00:03:46.413 --> 00:03:48.907
since driver updates have actually changed

00:03:48.907 --> 00:03:51.600
their performance
characteristics since launch.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.360
We've also revalidated any of
the numbers that we've reused

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.520
and we'll talk a bit more about our plans

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.342
for our GPU testing
methodology in a future video.

00:03:59.342 --> 00:04:01.860
Even with the full lineup,
"Shadow of the Tomb Raider"

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.223
has the 4070 TI within
about 8% of the RTX 3090 TI,

00:04:06.223 --> 00:04:09.000
which is a fairly impressive achievement

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.787
considering that it is
nominally a 70 series GPU.

00:04:12.787 --> 00:04:16.039
What's more impressive though
is that it gets handily beat

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:18.600
by the RX 7900 XT.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.788
Yes, yes, I'm aware that
that GPU costs $50 more,

00:04:22.788 --> 00:04:26.010
but it's the beginning of a pattern

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.963
that repeats itself first in "F1 2022,"

00:04:28.963 --> 00:04:32.465
where the 4070 TI gets stomped well beyond

00:04:32.465 --> 00:04:35.250
what that price difference would suggest.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.511
Starting the 4070 TI off
on pretty rocky ground.

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:41.580
"Hitman 3" continues this
floundering performance

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.670
with frame rates nearly
on par with the 3090 TI,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.180
but well off of AMD and the RTX 4080

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.980
that it used to share a name with,

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.510
and it's really more of the same story

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.397
with "Red Dead Redemption 2,"
"Assassin's Creed: Valhalla,"

00:04:54.397 --> 00:04:56.778
"Modern Warfare 2," and "Cyberpunk,"

00:04:56.778 --> 00:04:59.610
though "Cyberpunk" is more of a beat down

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.970
thanks to that game's memory
hungry nature, we think.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.490
Thankfully for Nvidia, "Forza Horizon 5"

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.680
gives the 4070 TI some breathing room

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.680
as it meets or beats the 7900 XT

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.170
and even beats the 3090 TI.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.980
Though it's still a good
13% off of the real 4080.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.918
It's a really good thing that
they changed that naming.

00:05:20.918 --> 00:05:23.730
For 4K gaming then, the RTX 4070 TI

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.941
ends up firmly behind both
of AMD's Radeon 7000 series

00:05:26.941 --> 00:05:31.830
and everything from Nvidia
from the 3090 TI and up,

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.440
which seems to suggest that
Nvidia basically looked at

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.434
3090 TI pricing on eBay and said,

00:05:36.434 --> 00:05:39.270
"hmm, about like that sounds good."

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.649
Maybe things will look
a bit better at 1440p.

00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:42.482
Eh?

00:05:42.482 --> 00:05:44.070
And they kind of do.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.297
Now that memory bandwidth
is less of an issue,

00:05:46.297 --> 00:05:48.720
"Shadow of the Tomb
Raider" sees the 4070 TI

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.553
pull ahead of the 3090 TI

00:05:50.553 --> 00:05:53.383
and close the gap on its bigger siblings.

00:05:53.383 --> 00:05:57.187
Though it still falls behind
AMD, if only slightly.

00:05:57.187 --> 00:05:59.880
"Hitman" sees the 4070
TI backsliding a little

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.910
in relation to the 7900
XT while maintaining

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.194
its performance improvement
over the 3090 TI,

00:06:05.194 --> 00:06:08.047
but this doesn't carry
over into "Cyberpunk,"

00:06:08.047 --> 00:06:11.250
"Modern Warfare 2," and
"Assassin's Creed: Valhalla,"

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.660
where we're looking at

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.597
pretty much the same relative
performance as our 4K tests.

00:06:16.597 --> 00:06:18.960
"Forza," however, picks up spectacularly

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.910
with the 4070 TI sitting
firmly in third place,

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:26.562
contributing to a generally
rosier picture at 1440p overall.

00:06:26.562 --> 00:06:29.640
With roughly equal
performance to a 3090 TI then

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.703
and a small enough gap from the 7900 XT,

00:06:32.703 --> 00:06:36.720
it looks almost compelling in
this overpriced GPU hellscape.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.810
And that's before we turn on ray tracing,

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.608
which works fine, right?

00:06:42.608 --> 00:06:45.120
I mean, we knew it would be superior

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.120
to the 7,900 XT since that's a stronghold

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.360
for Nvidia at this point,
but what was surprising

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.360
is that it wasn't by
as much as we expected.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:59.310
I was shocked to see the 7900
XTX actually outperform it

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.326
at 4K in everything except for "Cyberpunk"

00:07:02.326 --> 00:07:05.880
where the ray tracing cores
seemed to be doing a better job

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:06.870
potentially thanks to SER.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.620
At 1440p, things, again, get a bit better,

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.170
for the 4070 TI with only a mild loss

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.110
against the 7900 XTX in
"Shadow of the Tomb Raider"

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:19.027
and overall better performance
in the rest of the suite.

00:07:19.027 --> 00:07:21.510
"Cyberpunk" hits AMD even harder here,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:25.290
but that doesn't help the 4070
TI against its own brothers

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.270
who pull away significantly.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.530
That weak memory bandwidth

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.838
seems to be really holding it back here.

00:07:30.838 --> 00:07:34.260
Interestingly, when we turned on DLSS 3.0

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.680
to test if the 4070 TI's
diminished tensor core count

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.210
would prevent it from keeping up,

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:40.890
it did just fine in cyberpunk

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:42.510
with a similar performance uplift

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.910
to the 4080 and the 4090,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.450
but then we did not see the
same thing in "F1 2022."

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.190
We tried a bunch of
different settings here

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.650
and nothing we did change
to the outcome at all.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.450
We think this could be a driver issue

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:57.180
considering that "Cyberpunk"
of all games is fine.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.770
So we've reached out to Nvidia about it

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.210
to see what they have to say.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.740
If they respond in time for this video,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.930
we'll have their response
on screen right now,

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.648
and I'll just use this
time to tell you all about

00:08:06.648 --> 00:08:08.280
how delicious water tastes

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.230
from your LTTstore.com water bottle.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.400
It's great.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.380
In ray tracing then, the 4070 TI

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:16.338
is looking a lot like an RTX 3090 TI

00:08:16.338 --> 00:08:19.786
when we look at the overall
picture and as expected,

00:08:19.786 --> 00:08:22.050
ray tracing performance for the price

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.206
is superior to anything from Team Red.

00:08:24.206 --> 00:08:26.220
Unfortunately for Nvidia,

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.400
the vast majority of our
viewers have never once enabled

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.418
ray tracing and under 15%
of you actively use it.

00:08:33.418 --> 00:08:37.530
So at least for you guys, it
might not be the trump card

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.600
that they were hoping it would be.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.730
What might be though is productivity,

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.976
because remember, as much as
we like to talk about games,

00:08:43.976 --> 00:08:48.302
these GPUs for a lot of people,
end up used for work too.

00:08:48.302 --> 00:08:51.840
Starting with Nvidia's current
ave in the whole, Blender.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.770
The 4070 TI manages a
solid 43% improvement

00:08:55.770 --> 00:09:00.510
over its predecessor, but
falls behind the RTX 3090 TI

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.790
and especially the RTX 4080,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.290
with performance against the latter

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.150
being a mostly linear relationship

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.730
with the difference in
ray tracing core count.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.680
Things pick up a little with Procyon,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.410
with the video editing
suite giving the 4070 TI

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.230
very close performance
against the 3090 TI,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.520
though still slightly behind

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.890
both Radeon 7,000 series cards.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.525
Photo editing is interesting, however.

00:09:21.525 --> 00:09:23.160
Most of the results are within

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.920
a couple of percentage
points, which seems to suggest

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.727
that any modern GPU will be sufficient,

00:09:27.727 --> 00:09:30.190
but this doesn't tell the whole story.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.820
As more machine learning
effects and filters

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.230
trickle into your workflow,

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:36.330
you can expect to see
these numbers change,

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.563
and it could be a pretty
solid advantage for Nvidia.

00:09:39.563 --> 00:09:42.769
Da Vinci Resolve gives the
4070 TIs end bank encoders

00:09:42.769 --> 00:09:45.240
a run for their money
with what looks to be

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.710
a memory bandwidth bottleneck,

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.460
slowing it down considerably
next to its bigger siblings.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.178
It manages to be a little bit faster

00:09:51.178 --> 00:09:53.110
than its bandwidth
difference would suggest,

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.146
but this unfortunately means
that out of our test suite

00:09:56.146 --> 00:10:01.146
it only really beats the
RTX 3060 in export speed.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.740
Topaz AI though is a better
story for the 4070 TI

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.300
where it's enhanced core architecture

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.378
makes shorter work of this GPGP workload

00:10:08.378 --> 00:10:10.683
than even the RTX 3090 TI,

00:10:10.683 --> 00:10:14.153
though AMD is the one who
ends up stealing the show here

00:10:14.153 --> 00:10:16.697
with even the last gen 6900 XT

00:10:16.697 --> 00:10:18.627
putting up comparable numbers.

00:10:18.627 --> 00:10:21.840
The spec suites are another
strong suit for Nvidia

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.550
ever since the 20 series,

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:26.709
and the RTX 4070 TI trades
blows with the 3090 TI

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:29.736
in a lot of these tests,
which is a good thing.

00:10:29.736 --> 00:10:33.270
Unfortunately though there
are cases like 3DS Max

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:36.780
and SolidWorks that give
the 4070 TI some trouble,

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.837
likely again, thanks to its
relative shortage of cores

00:10:39.837 --> 00:10:41.880
and limited memory bandwidth.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.946
However, energy and medical
end up being standout wins

00:10:44.946 --> 00:10:47.375
for the 4070 TI and in all cases,

00:10:47.375 --> 00:10:50.134
it's substantially
faster than the RTX 3080,

00:10:50.134 --> 00:10:52.687
let alone the 3070 TI.

00:10:52.687 --> 00:10:54.810
That ignores AMD, of course, though,

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.120
whose cards all spank it across the board

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.220
with the exception of 3DS Max.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.580
Siemens NX as usual will be ignored

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:03.420
in our final performance tallies

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.800
since this version is old enough

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.260
to not have proper
support for Nvidia GPUs,

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:08.359
but we're putting the data in here

00:11:08.359 --> 00:11:10.350
in case you're interested.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.329
Finally, the 4070 TI
outperforms the RTX 3090 TI

00:11:13.329 --> 00:11:15.678
in every part of SPECworkstation,

00:11:15.678 --> 00:11:19.290
although as with Viewperf,
AMD maintains big wins

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:22.020
over all of Nvidia's cards
across most of the suite

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.060
with the exceptions of
media and entertainment

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.680
and GPU compute.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.041
So much for that stronghold.

00:11:27.041 --> 00:11:30.088
Overall performance looks like a 3090 TI,

00:11:30.088 --> 00:11:33.779
but at a lower cost and
hopefully a lower power draw,

00:11:33.779 --> 00:11:36.270
but it really does depend
on what you're doing.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.700
If you're into 3D
rendering or GPU compute,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:41.550
you're gonna get a lot
more bang out of a 4070 TI

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.640
than the 3090 TI, which could
make it an attractive option

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.760
for the price and
hopefully the power draw.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.500
If you're into video editing however,

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.010
it's not a worthy upgrade
over even the 3070 TI

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.000
and you might want to consider

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.631
a 7,000 series Radeon instead
if you need a new GP today,

00:11:57.631 --> 00:12:00.748
preferably one with hardware
AV encoding support.

00:12:00.748 --> 00:12:03.090
So if we're looking at an RTX 3090

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.322
or a 3090 TI equivalent here,

00:12:05.322 --> 00:12:08.756
surely at the 4070 TI should
be more energy efficient too.

00:12:08.756 --> 00:12:12.300
Well, in MSI Kombustor,
it pulled the lowest

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:13.783
out of not only the 40 series family,

00:12:13.783 --> 00:12:17.280
but also when compared to
AMD's price competitors,

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.639
hovering at around 280
watts most of the time

00:12:19.639 --> 00:12:23.268
and never breaking past
its 285 watt rating.

00:12:23.268 --> 00:12:26.970
When we switch to a more
real world test, "F1 22,"

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.770
the story stays pretty much the same,

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:32.160
this time with the card
hovering at around 275 watts.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.440
That is well with in
spec even though our card

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.677
is a factory overclocked model.

00:12:36.677 --> 00:12:38.700
It's worth noting by the way guys,

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.153
that in our Radiant RX
7,900 series review,

00:12:41.153 --> 00:12:43.620
we made a mistake when we changed the way

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:44.820
that we collected our data

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.550
and we initially showed
total graphics power

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.100
and total board power in the same chart.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.110
Those measurements are not comparable.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.832
So this time we are strictly
using total board power

00:12:54.832 --> 00:12:56.760
and in the future we're planning to use

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:58.620
external equipment for better accuracy

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.350
than board sensors or Nvidia's P-cap.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.790
Stay tuned for that.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.431
When we zoom in on our
power consumption results

00:13:04.431 --> 00:13:06.908
for "Forza Horizon Five" at 1440p,

00:13:06.908 --> 00:13:09.136
the power consumption
without a frame rate cap

00:13:09.136 --> 00:13:11.808
is pretty much in line with the RTX 4080,

00:13:11.808 --> 00:13:13.968
which actually is not a great thing

00:13:13.968 --> 00:13:17.528
considering that it ran
about five to 10 FPSs slower.

00:13:17.528 --> 00:13:20.670
However, when we cap the
frame rate to a hundred,

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.100
it actually lines up so perfectly,

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.955
I almost thought the graph
only had three lines.

00:13:24.955 --> 00:13:27.236
AMD's cards by comparison ran about

00:13:27.236 --> 00:13:30.028
30 to 50 watts higher most of the time.

00:13:30.028 --> 00:13:33.207
That works out to about
17 watts per frame less

00:13:33.207 --> 00:13:36.313
than the Radeon 7900 XT when it's uncapped

00:13:36.313 --> 00:13:39.600
and a good 35 watts when capped.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.089
Not a bad efficiency story,

00:13:41.089 --> 00:13:44.940
and no high idle power consumption bugs

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:46.590
when connected to different monitors

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.740
like we saw with AMD's 7000 series.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.380
Now thermals are gonna be
a difficult conversation,

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.223
since there isn't a like a reference

00:13:54.223 --> 00:13:57.930
or a founder's edition of the RTX 4070 TI

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:00.583
with a standard cooler for us to test.

00:14:00.583 --> 00:14:02.130
With that in mind then,

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:03.960
take these results with a grain of salt.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:05.640
Starting with Kombustor.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.760
It's not looking great
for our tough OC edition

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.070
of the 4070 TI, which quickly ramps up

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:14.430
past 80 degrees and holds
there throughout our run.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:18.450
This is well above the RTX
4080 with its founders cooler

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:20.737
and more in line with the 4090.

00:14:20.737 --> 00:14:23.910
"F1 22" pushes the 4070 TI even higher

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:26.024
relative to the 4090, sticking again

00:14:26.024 --> 00:14:28.125
to around the 80 degree mark.

00:14:28.125 --> 00:14:30.300
To be clear, this isn't
the end of the world,

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.000
as long as the core
clocks are high and stable

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.350
at these temperatures.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.900
And high they probably will be.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.420
I suspect that the only 4070 TIs

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.340
that you will find readily
available at launch

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.650
will be factory overclocked like ours,

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:45.450
which managed among the highest clocks

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:47.490
that we've ever seen on a GPU,

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:50.530
even though it can't maintain
full-boosting Kombustor

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:54.598
and has a little bit more
wobble than the RTX 4080.

00:14:54.598 --> 00:14:58.320
It's worth saying again though
that your mileage will vary

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.017
depending on a given card's
cooling setup and B bios.

00:15:02.017 --> 00:15:04.920
"F1 22" is a more realistic load

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.200
and sees that variation calm down a bit

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.780
with the 4070 TI with it regularly hitting

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:13.500
our factory overclocked cards
2.7 gigahertz boost clock.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:15.240
But again, your mileage may vary

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.040
once the first summer heat wave strikes,

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.510
because as you guys saw,

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:21.000
we're flying pretty close
to the sun on our thermals,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.300
and that's in a temperature
controlled room.

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.957
As the saying goes, there's no
such thing as a bad product,

00:15:26.957 --> 00:15:28.890
only a bad price.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.771
So it's time to talk about that.

00:15:30.771 --> 00:15:33.660
Originally announced at $900,

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:37.463
as the RTX 4080 12 gig,
the current starting price,

00:15:37.463 --> 00:15:41.287
now that it is the 4070 TI, is $800.

00:15:41.287 --> 00:15:43.455
That's a hundred dollars less.

00:15:43.455 --> 00:15:47.130
But not only is that $200 higher

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:50.281
than the RTX 3070 TI MSRP was at launch,

00:15:50.281 --> 00:15:54.291
that price only barely puts
it in a competitive position

00:15:54.291 --> 00:15:58.443
against the Radeon RX7900
XT in typical raster gaming,

00:15:58.443 --> 00:16:02.460
and it's not even
guaranteed to net you 60 FPS

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.109
if you do turn on ray tracing,

00:16:04.109 --> 00:16:07.230
making some flavor of DLS a necessity

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.193
unless you want dial back the settings.

00:16:09.193 --> 00:16:14.193
On an $850 card, it's still
a lot for what, by its name,

00:16:14.678 --> 00:16:17.097
should be a high mid-range GPU,

00:16:17.097 --> 00:16:20.970
not a step-down enthusiast class one.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.960
So how does Nvidia justify
its astronomical price tag

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:28.451
for a 70 series GPU
that as we said before,

00:16:28.451 --> 00:16:31.309
really seems more like a 60 super?

00:16:31.309 --> 00:16:34.020
To put it bluntly, they don't.

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.971
And as far as they're concerned,
they shouldn't have to.

00:16:36.971 --> 00:16:40.080
I mean, you think they
renamed the RTX 4070 TI

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.310
out of the goodness of their hearts

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.649
and it was a big win?

00:16:42.649 --> 00:16:43.950
No!

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:45.842
Up until literally just last week,

00:16:45.842 --> 00:16:48.240
it was still up in the air whether Nvidia

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.027
would drop the price at all

00:16:50.027 --> 00:16:52.898
compared to the announced $900 price

00:16:52.898 --> 00:16:55.410
for the RTX 4080 12 gig.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.350
It took their business
partners begging and pleading

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:01.260
with them to make that
happen, and that was in spite

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.755
of the reportedly atrocious
sales figures for the RTX 4080

00:17:04.755 --> 00:17:07.946
and the Radeon 7900 XT's existence

00:17:07.946 --> 00:17:10.367
at that very same price point.

00:17:10.367 --> 00:17:14.670
It's good they yielded, but
they still have more work to do.

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:17.188
Nvidia reportedly offered
partners some compensation

00:17:17.188 --> 00:17:19.349
for the work involved in the rebranding,

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:21.810
but at this point we
haven't heard confirmation

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.814
that they intend to do the
same for this new MSRP.

00:17:24.814 --> 00:17:26.913
That means that if this chart

00:17:26.913 --> 00:17:30.090
showing the meager scraps of margin

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.438
that Nvidia leads for its
partners is to be believed,

00:17:32.438 --> 00:17:36.360
any MSRP cards that
you see are going to be

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.933
very limited in stock and
essentially just for show

00:17:39.933 --> 00:17:42.281
so that media like us will be suckers

00:17:42.281 --> 00:17:46.140
and talk about how this card is 799.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.120
Board makers, like any other business,

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:50.070
are not gonna go go out
of their way to lose money

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.153
if Nvidia hasn't adjusted the pricing.

00:17:52.153 --> 00:17:55.296
Asus's top end strict 4070 TI then

00:17:55.296 --> 00:17:59.880
is just $150 less than a
4080 founder's edition,

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:02.881
making even that card
look good by comparison.

00:18:02.881 --> 00:18:06.210
Reports from ASUS
suggest that the tough OC

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.138
will be the most plentiful skew,

00:18:08.138 --> 00:18:09.780
but from conversations we've had

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.550
with Memory Express here in Canada,

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.050
that's actually been the opposite

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:14.940
for the rest of the 40 series lineup

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.460
where almost no tough cards exist.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.920
So what it looks like is Nvidia

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:22.770
is pointing at the price saying,
"but it can't be expensive.

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:24.090
We set it at 799.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:24.923
See?"

00:18:24.923 --> 00:18:27.390
But the partners have absolutely no way

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:29.012
of achieving that in the real world.

00:18:29.012 --> 00:18:31.620
I'm having a hard time
figuring out Nvidia's game here

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:32.760
to be honest with you.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.910
The GPU market has reportedly
hit a 20 year sales low

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.073
thanks to a combination of high prices,

00:18:38.073 --> 00:18:42.180
the mining crash, and extenuating
economic circumstances,

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:45.263
and yet Nvidia seems determined
to pour water on the fire

00:18:45.263 --> 00:18:48.810
expecting a huge premium even on products

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:52.914
in the high slash mid-tier
of their product stack.

00:18:52.914 --> 00:18:54.360
I mean, don't forget.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.670
As much as we focus on desktop GPUs

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:58.140
and PC gaming on this channel,

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.840
the real competition for a card like this

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:04.282
could easily be an Xbox or a PlayStation 5

00:19:04.282 --> 00:19:06.390
either of which would frankly be

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.720
a much more compelling
value to the average gamer.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.810
And I feel like Nvidia, if
they don't turn things around,

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:15.572
is at risk of losing PC gaming customers

00:19:15.572 --> 00:19:18.044
long term to the console side.

00:19:18.044 --> 00:19:20.190
It's starting to almost look like

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:23.850
a Microsoftian good, bad, good cycle

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.520
that they've been on for
the last few years here.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.154
10 series is great, 20
series was a wet fart,

00:19:29.154 --> 00:19:32.193
30 series kickstarted a whole
new generation of PC gamers

00:19:32.193 --> 00:19:34.896
in spite of the pandemic price hikes,

00:19:34.896 --> 00:19:38.471
and as product development
cycles run for longer and longer

00:19:38.471 --> 00:19:40.380
with each new generation,

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:44.115
the impact of this kind of
please people 50% of the time,

00:19:44.115 --> 00:19:46.097
piss them off 50% of the time

00:19:46.097 --> 00:19:48.330
starts to become more dramatic.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.838
And Nvidia clearly hasn't
learned their lesson yet.

00:19:50.838 --> 00:19:53.190
I mean, Apple and Tesla have proven

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:54.780
that people will still buy your product

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:58.200
even if you treat them badly,
but that only holds true

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.990
if you have the absolute
best products on the market.

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:02.395
It doesn't build loyalty.

00:20:02.395 --> 00:20:05.036
So this strategy around the 4070 TI

00:20:05.036 --> 00:20:10.036
and especially the 4080 clearly
isn't it for the long term.

00:20:10.413 --> 00:20:15.210
It is time for Nvidia to focus
on squeezing out margins less

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.518
and delighting customers more.

00:20:17.518 --> 00:20:21.390
Now, AMD is not innocent here to be clear.

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:25.697
While the RX 7900 series is
a better value than the 4080,

00:20:25.697 --> 00:20:29.745
the 4070 TI is content to
price itself right in line

00:20:29.745 --> 00:20:33.043
with its performance against the 7900 XT,

00:20:33.043 --> 00:20:35.580
a card that also should probably be more

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:39.870
like $700 considering its
80 class performance level.

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:42.960
Nvidia enabled AMD then
to charge more themselves

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:44.970
through their own astronomical pricing,

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:48.720
and AMD is in turn enabling
Nvidia to normalize those prices

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.243
by providing a comparably fair price

00:20:51.243 --> 00:20:53.400
on this step down product,

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.830
which Nvidia can then compete with

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:58.080
as they both run down the stack.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.420
As long as this continues
and as long as Nvidia

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:02.040
keeps trying to kill Moore's law

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.600
on the collective consciousness,

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:05.645
things are never going to change.

00:21:05.645 --> 00:21:07.590
Nowhere is this attitude more apparent

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:10.800
than Nvidia's positioning
for the RTX 4070 TI,

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.351
not as competition for AMD or even against

00:21:13.351 --> 00:21:15.822
a 30 series card, but as an upgrade

00:21:15.822 --> 00:21:19.631
from 20 series and 10 series GPUs.

00:21:19.631 --> 00:21:23.220
And I mean, yes, it is an upgrade,

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:24.690
but it would also be an upgrade

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.051
over a toaster or a literal potato,

00:21:27.051 --> 00:21:29.700
especially if that potato
is seven years old.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.370
I mean, even an ARC
GPU would be an upgrade

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.770
over a 10 series most of the time.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.169
So what's your point, Nvidia?

00:21:37.169 --> 00:21:38.924
Final word then.

00:21:38.924 --> 00:21:42.630
It's a good product at a
bad price from a company

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:46.290
that is failing more and
more to hide its ugly nature.

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:50.250
You're gonna buy it, you
always do, and you know what?

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:51.960
You'll probably be happy with it,

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.820
but I don't have to be happy about that.

